notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-10T16:25:14.060Z","harness:claude-code","Another member of this wall-clock class, observed 2026-08-10, and this one sits in a release gate rather than in a spec.\n\nThe SDK entrypoint import-cost gate compares each of ten entrypoints against a per-entrypoint millisecond budget. Run inside the composite static quality command it failed twice, and it named different entrypoints each time: first the query entrypoint at 176ms against a 172ms budget, then the merge entrypoint at 201ms against 195ms together with the testing entrypoint at 523ms against 445ms. Run standalone on the identical working tree it passed twice in a row, reporting all ten entrypoints verified. The working tree contained no change under src or sdk at all, so the measured quantity could not have moved; only the machine load did.\n\nThe signature worth recording is that the failing member of the set rotates between runs. A real budget regression names the same entrypoint every time, because the cost is a property of the module graph. A load-induced failure names whichever entrypoint happened to be measured while the machine was busiest, so the identity of the failing entrypoint is close to random across runs. That is a cheap discriminator available to any reader of a red log, and it does not require re-running anything: one failing entrypoint reproducing across runs is a regression, a different one each run is this class.\n\nThe wider point for this item is that the class now spans three surfaces. It was first recorded in a spec that decides from elapsed time, then in two gates that flake under concurrent load, and now in a gate whose thresholds are absolute wall-clock milliseconds per entrypoint with no tolerance for the load the composite command itself creates. A per-entrypoint absolute budget measured inside a command that runs many other gates is measuring the harness as much as the entrypoint. Whatever fix this item lands should be evaluated against the gate case and not only the spec case, because the gate case is the one that can block a release.\n\nNot filed separately, consistent with the standing disposition that load-induced gate flakes belong to this item rather than to new ones."
body: ""
